1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,820
This video is part of
a multipart series

2
00:00:02,820 --> 00:00:05,655
talking about bootloader
and Device Trees.

3
00:00:05,655 --> 00:00:07,710
We want to do with this video is

4
00:00:07,710 --> 00:00:09,540
understand the role
of the bootloader,

5
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:12,060
understand the role
of Device Trees.

6
00:00:12,060 --> 00:00:13,800
Also let's go through

7
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:18,340
some simple and then more
complex bootloader examples.

8
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:21,275
Bootloader at a high level,

9
00:00:21,275 --> 00:00:22,680
what we want to do is initialize

10
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:24,555
the system at some basic level.

11
00:00:24,555 --> 00:00:26,250
Really what we want to do is get

12
00:00:26,250 --> 00:00:28,200
far enough that we
can load the kernel.

13
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:30,420
In the beginning
of the semester,

14
00:00:30,420 --> 00:00:33,179
we've been using QEMU
for demonstrating

15
00:00:33,179 --> 00:00:36,210
a lot of how the kernel works,

16
00:00:36,210 --> 00:00:38,400
and how we can use
the Linux kernel,

17
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:41,190
in both kernel space
and user space.

18
00:00:41,190 --> 00:00:43,415
We've been able to ignore

19
00:00:43,415 --> 00:00:45,560
how you get as far as
loading the kernel,

20
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:47,780
but that's really part of
the job of the bootloader.

21
00:00:47,780 --> 00:00:49,865
As we start to work
with hardware,

22
00:00:49,865 --> 00:00:52,295
the bootloader becomes more
important because it's

23
00:00:52,295 --> 00:00:53,390
a necessary piece of

24
00:00:53,390 --> 00:00:55,525
being able to work
with the Linux kernel.

25
00:00:55,525 --> 00:00:57,770
You often are going
to be starting with

26
00:00:57,770 --> 00:01:00,830
a single CPU core and a
set of on-chip memory,

27
00:01:00,830 --> 00:01:02,960
which for more
complicated parts,

28
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:04,805
is probably only a small subset

29
00:01:04,805 --> 00:01:08,010
of the overall
hardware solution.

30
00:01:08,170 --> 00:01:10,400
What a bootloader is going to do

31
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:13,250
is what's known
as bootstrapping.

32
00:01:13,250 --> 00:01:15,335
In other words, starting up,

33
00:01:15,335 --> 00:01:17,405
initializing some
piece of hardware,

34
00:01:17,405 --> 00:01:19,310
using that hardware
to initialize

35
00:01:19,310 --> 00:01:21,200
the next hardware
in the process,

36
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:23,240
and loading the different
startup software

37
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:25,280
that it needs to completely
initialize the system,

38
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:26,600
and run the Linux kernel,

39
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:28,615
loading all of that in phases.

40
00:01:28,615 --> 00:01:29,840
Some of the different phases,

41
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:31,805
including setting up DRAM,

42
00:01:31,805 --> 00:01:33,410
setting up access to storage,

43
00:01:33,410 --> 00:01:35,225
whatever you're
persistent storage is.

44
00:01:35,225 --> 00:01:37,890
Then loading the
kernel into RAM.

45
00:01:38,870 --> 00:01:41,030
We'll also talk a
little bit about

46
00:01:41,030 --> 00:01:43,460
Device Trees because they're

47
00:01:43,460 --> 00:01:46,010
also important for
the boot process.

48
00:01:46,010 --> 00:01:50,495
Before around February 2013
and kernel version 3.8,

49
00:01:50,495 --> 00:01:54,015
the platform implementation for

50
00:01:54,015 --> 00:01:56,650
Linux kernel was
either discovered at

51
00:01:56,650 --> 00:02:00,145
runtime or was hard-coded
directly into the kernel.

52
00:02:00,145 --> 00:02:03,160
This became unwieldy and

53
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:05,330
was less useful for
embedded devices.

54
00:02:05,330 --> 00:02:08,875
It was more appropriate
for things like PCs,

55
00:02:08,875 --> 00:02:11,560
where hardware varied widely.

56
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:13,390
You need to be able
to basically auto

57
00:02:13,390 --> 00:02:15,250
discover everything
from the kernel,

58
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:18,310
as opposed to an
embedded or ARM system,

59
00:02:18,310 --> 00:02:20,530
where you pretty much know
exactly what hardware

60
00:02:20,530 --> 00:02:22,750
you're going to
have at boot time.

61
00:02:22,750 --> 00:02:25,265
You also might be a little
more resource-constrained,

62
00:02:25,265 --> 00:02:27,490
in terms of how many different
variations of drivers

63
00:02:27,490 --> 00:02:30,040
you would want to support,

64
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:32,210
based on the hardware.

65
00:02:32,420 --> 00:02:34,540
To solve this problem,

66
00:02:34,540 --> 00:02:38,450
the Linux kernel developers
started replacing

67
00:02:38,450 --> 00:02:41,490
this functionality
by a functionality

68
00:02:41,490 --> 00:02:42,900
called Device Trees,

69
00:02:42,900 --> 00:02:44,480
especially on ARM, which is

70
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:47,765
a pretty big part of
the embedded solution.

71
00:02:47,765 --> 00:02:50,060
Device Trees have been the go-to

72
00:02:50,060 --> 00:02:53,035
way of configuring
devices in Linux.

73
00:02:53,035 --> 00:02:55,820
It really represents
the system and

74
00:02:55,820 --> 00:02:58,670
the associated devices
that make up the system,

75
00:02:58,670 --> 00:03:01,190
as a collection of components
in a hierarchy tree.

76
00:03:01,190 --> 00:03:03,805
That's where the name
Device Tree comes from.

77
00:03:03,805 --> 00:03:06,770
It includes these properties
and key-value pairs

78
00:03:06,770 --> 00:03:10,235
with a form of a name that's
assigned to know value.

79
00:03:10,235 --> 00:03:12,530
The Mastering Embedded
Linux Programming,

80
00:03:12,530 --> 00:03:14,710
Chapter 3 goes over
these in more detail,

81
00:03:14,710 --> 00:03:16,910
but you can see the things
that we can specify

82
00:03:16,910 --> 00:03:19,430
here with the Device Tree.

83
00:03:19,430 --> 00:03:22,400
We can talk about where
memory is located,

84
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:25,430
and how it fits in the
overall memory space.

85
00:03:25,430 --> 00:03:27,470
We can describe the CPUs that we

86
00:03:27,470 --> 00:03:30,505
have connected to the system.

87
00:03:30,505 --> 00:03:33,335
We can describe things like
the interrupt controller,

88
00:03:33,335 --> 00:03:36,215
what location it's
located at in memory,

89
00:03:36,215 --> 00:03:38,375
what interrupts are supported,

90
00:03:38,375 --> 00:03:40,295
and how those are being used.

91
00:03:40,295 --> 00:03:43,860
In this case, for an LCD device.

92
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:47,960
The ARM bootloader and kernel

93
00:03:47,960 --> 00:03:51,280
requires a Device Tree to
be compiled before using.

94
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:52,820
You can think of it very similar

95
00:03:52,820 --> 00:03:54,335
to what we do with a C file.

96
00:03:54,335 --> 00:03:56,510
This would be the source
code implementation,

97
00:03:56,510 --> 00:03:58,085
the human-readable portion.

98
00:03:58,085 --> 00:04:01,280
Then you go through a compiled
step to turn that into

99
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:05,230
a binary that the bootloader
or kernel can understand.

100
00:04:05,230 --> 00:04:09,020
The binary would be a DTB,

101
00:04:09,020 --> 00:04:10,220
which is often referred to as

102
00:04:10,220 --> 00:04:13,060
either a Device Tree Binary
or a Device Tree Blob,

103
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:15,935
and the compilation step,

104
00:04:15,935 --> 00:04:18,740
instead of using GCC for C code,

105
00:04:18,740 --> 00:04:22,195
we'd use DTC, which is
the Device Tree Compiler.

106
00:04:22,195 --> 00:04:24,750
The result, it could

107
00:04:24,750 --> 00:04:25,830
be referred to as

108
00:04:25,830 --> 00:04:27,625
a Device Tree Binary,
Device Tree Blob.

109
00:04:27,625 --> 00:04:29,270
You might also see
it referred to as

110
00:04:29,270 --> 00:04:32,610
a Flattened Device
Tree or an FDT.

111
00:04:34,130 --> 00:04:37,720
Let's talk a little bit
about how bootloader

112
00:04:37,720 --> 00:04:40,570
will work from a
conceptual point of view.

113
00:04:40,570 --> 00:04:43,300
First, starting with the
simple bootloader example.

114
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:45,190
This is probably something
you're familiar with,

115
00:04:45,190 --> 00:04:47,650
if you've done any embedded
development at all,

116
00:04:47,650 --> 00:04:52,915
and use some low-resource
common embedded product.

117
00:04:52,915 --> 00:04:55,370
There's typically
a reset vector.

118
00:04:55,370 --> 00:04:59,160
You got a reset vector
that boots into NOR flash.

119
00:04:59,160 --> 00:05:02,085
That NOR flash
probably sets up RAM,

120
00:05:02,085 --> 00:05:05,130
sets up the boot,
and run from RAM.

121
00:05:05,130 --> 00:05:06,610
In some cases, maybe there's

122
00:05:06,610 --> 00:05:08,140
a little more complexity
in the NOR flash.

123
00:05:08,140 --> 00:05:10,105
It has to deal with
something like DRAM.

124
00:05:10,105 --> 00:05:12,340
But ultimately, it's still
a pretty simple scenario,

125
00:05:12,340 --> 00:05:15,330
where a flat linear
addressing space that's all

126
00:05:15,330 --> 00:05:19,005
addressed from the code that
starts with the NOR flash,

127
00:05:19,005 --> 00:05:21,715
and is pretty
straightforward to config,

128
00:05:21,715 --> 00:05:23,720
and run from a
bootloader perspective.

129
00:05:23,720 --> 00:05:26,760
Not a lot of work for the
bootloader to do here.

130
00:05:26,900 --> 00:05:29,750
Why can't we just always
use a simple bootloader?

131
00:05:29,750 --> 00:05:32,285
That one looks nice. Why
would we just always do that?

132
00:05:32,285 --> 00:05:35,030
Well, it turns out that
it gets a little more

133
00:05:35,030 --> 00:05:38,075
complex with different parts,

134
00:05:38,075 --> 00:05:40,910
especially with the
higher performance parts

135
00:05:40,910 --> 00:05:43,942
that you might want to use an
embedded Linux environment.

136
00:05:43,942 --> 00:05:47,350
One of the complexities
associated with NAND flash.

137
00:05:47,350 --> 00:05:49,915
With NAND flash
versus NOR flash,

138
00:05:49,915 --> 00:05:52,420
and this is covered in other
courses you've probably

139
00:05:52,420 --> 00:05:55,795
taken or will be
taking in more detail,

140
00:05:55,795 --> 00:05:59,110
the content is no longer
linearly addressable,

141
00:05:59,110 --> 00:06:01,870
meaning that you
can't just jump to

142
00:06:01,870 --> 00:06:03,190
an address and assume
you'll be able

143
00:06:03,190 --> 00:06:05,725
to read the content
of the flash.

144
00:06:05,725 --> 00:06:08,080
You probably need to
use something like ECC,

145
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:10,375
support recovery of bad blocks,

146
00:06:10,375 --> 00:06:12,054
and so all this requires

147
00:06:12,054 --> 00:06:14,620
some additional software work to

148
00:06:14,620 --> 00:06:16,120
support and potentially some

149
00:06:16,120 --> 00:06:17,795
additional hardware
work to support,

150
00:06:17,795 --> 00:06:20,740
so it makes the bootloader
a little more complicated.

151
00:06:20,740 --> 00:06:24,130
There's also just general
complexities of the hardware,

152
00:06:24,130 --> 00:06:27,535
the multiple cores
or cores that need

153
00:06:27,535 --> 00:06:29,260
other hardware components like

154
00:06:29,260 --> 00:06:32,005
DRAM brought up before
they're able to function.

155
00:06:32,005 --> 00:06:35,050
There's more work that
needs to happen before we

156
00:06:35,050 --> 00:06:38,870
can actually get to the point
that we could start Linux.

157
00:06:39,030 --> 00:06:42,610
If we want to go through a
more complicated example,

158
00:06:42,610 --> 00:06:43,990
and this is again covered in

159
00:06:43,990 --> 00:06:46,750
your Mastering Embedded Linux
Programming Chapter 3 book.

160
00:06:46,750 --> 00:06:48,760
But some of the different
components here that we'll be

161
00:06:48,760 --> 00:06:51,130
talking through in this
more complicated example.

162
00:06:51,130 --> 00:06:53,770
Let's say you've got an
on-board or external DRAM,

163
00:06:53,770 --> 00:06:56,830
so you've got a dynamic RAM
that you have to refresh,

164
00:06:56,830 --> 00:06:59,140
probably need some
hardware support for that,

165
00:06:59,140 --> 00:07:01,525
there will be a
tertiary program loader

166
00:07:01,525 --> 00:07:02,800
that's associated with that,

167
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:05,590
that's the third stage
of our boot process.

168
00:07:05,590 --> 00:07:08,740
Then you might have an
on-board or external flash,

169
00:07:08,740 --> 00:07:10,390
and maybe that flash
is NAND flash,

170
00:07:10,390 --> 00:07:11,755
instead of NOR flash,

171
00:07:11,755 --> 00:07:13,390
so that means that
there's some extra

172
00:07:13,390 --> 00:07:14,500
work that you'll have
to do to be able

173
00:07:14,500 --> 00:07:18,985
to read and access
that NAND flash.

174
00:07:18,985 --> 00:07:20,860
The things that
would be stored in

175
00:07:20,860 --> 00:07:22,405
there would be things like

176
00:07:22,405 --> 00:07:24,190
compressed images

177
00:07:24,190 --> 00:07:26,065
on the flattened device
tree that we talked about,

178
00:07:26,065 --> 00:07:28,165
the device tree,
the kernel image,

179
00:07:28,165 --> 00:07:30,205
maybe an initramfs image that

180
00:07:30,205 --> 00:07:32,935
will be the early bootloader
portion of your kernel.

181
00:07:32,935 --> 00:07:35,035
Maybe one or more
root filesystems,

182
00:07:35,035 --> 00:07:38,650
and probably some
images related to

183
00:07:38,650 --> 00:07:43,640
the program loaders like
the SPL or TPL image.

184
00:07:43,680 --> 00:07:47,470
Then you might also
have an on-board SOC.

185
00:07:47,470 --> 00:07:49,870
Some base system that

186
00:07:49,870 --> 00:07:52,480
is able to start from
the reset vectors,

187
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:54,490
so the part that implements

188
00:07:54,490 --> 00:07:55,595
the simple bootloader that we

189
00:07:55,595 --> 00:07:57,335
talked about in the first slide.

190
00:07:57,335 --> 00:07:59,690
You'd have some type
of reset vector,

191
00:07:59,690 --> 00:08:03,740
some storage, and in a lot
of cases, it might be ROM.

192
00:08:03,740 --> 00:08:05,470
This might not be something
that's programmable,

193
00:08:05,470 --> 00:08:08,915
it might be a fixed thing
that's built into the part.

194
00:08:08,915 --> 00:08:11,890
Then possibly a SRAM with

195
00:08:11,890 --> 00:08:14,855
secondary program loader
as a part of that SOC,

196
00:08:14,855 --> 00:08:16,990
so a small amount of RAM that's

197
00:08:16,990 --> 00:08:21,560
available specifically for
the purposes of initial boot.

198
00:08:22,170 --> 00:08:25,390
Going through how all this
could work together in

199
00:08:25,390 --> 00:08:27,964
a more complicated
bootloader example,

200
00:08:27,964 --> 00:08:29,800
you can have the reset
vector boot into

201
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:32,095
ROM and then ROM
could initialize

202
00:08:32,095 --> 00:08:36,400
SPL or a small SRAM area.

203
00:08:36,400 --> 00:08:40,250
Do the work to enable
access to NAND,

204
00:08:40,250 --> 00:08:43,295
get the content of the
SPL image from NAND,

205
00:08:43,295 --> 00:08:47,345
copy that into our SRAM for
secondary program loader.

206
00:08:47,345 --> 00:08:50,080
A lot of times this
content could be

207
00:08:50,080 --> 00:08:52,960
stored in a special
portion of NAND flash,

208
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:55,060
for instance, a section

209
00:08:55,060 --> 00:08:57,055
where you don't need
to read with ECC,

210
00:08:57,055 --> 00:08:58,930
and so it's a little bit easier

211
00:08:58,930 --> 00:09:01,650
from a software
perspective to access it.

212
00:09:01,650 --> 00:09:04,345
It could also be that the SPL

213
00:09:04,345 --> 00:09:07,855
could get transferred in
from an external interface.

214
00:09:07,855 --> 00:09:10,900
For instance, you might be
able to have the ROM be

215
00:09:10,900 --> 00:09:13,540
able to access over
USB and possibly

216
00:09:13,540 --> 00:09:16,630
pull the secondary program
loader over something like

217
00:09:16,630 --> 00:09:17,920
a USB connection as

218
00:09:17,920 --> 00:09:20,330
an alternative to reading
out of NAND flash.

219
00:09:20,330 --> 00:09:22,180
But one way or another,
it will be the job of

220
00:09:22,180 --> 00:09:24,640
that phase 1 ROM code to get

221
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:27,000
the secondary program
loader into RAM and be

222
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:30,530
able to run the secondary
program loader.

223
00:09:31,320 --> 00:09:34,720
Then as a part of running the
secondary program loader,

224
00:09:34,720 --> 00:09:37,410
now we probably have
enough software support to

225
00:09:37,410 --> 00:09:40,220
initialize the hardware that
we need to access the DRAM,

226
00:09:40,220 --> 00:09:42,790
and we'll probably also be able

227
00:09:42,790 --> 00:09:45,325
to complete initialization
of the NAND flash,

228
00:09:45,325 --> 00:09:49,645
be able to use ECC and read
more content from the NAND.

229
00:09:49,645 --> 00:09:53,170
The job of a secondary
program loader might be

230
00:09:53,170 --> 00:09:56,185
to load the tertiary
program loader.

231
00:09:56,185 --> 00:09:58,160
Give us access to
the NAND flash,

232
00:09:58,160 --> 00:10:00,065
give us access to the DRAM,

233
00:10:00,065 --> 00:10:03,490
and have enough capability to

234
00:10:03,490 --> 00:10:07,690
load that tertiary program
loader out of NAND flash,

235
00:10:07,690 --> 00:10:11,600
put it into DRAM, and then
jump and run from DRAM.

236
00:10:12,210 --> 00:10:15,845
Again, the TPL could be content

237
00:10:15,845 --> 00:10:18,970
received over USB or
some external interface,

238
00:10:18,970 --> 00:10:22,840
or maybe it could be content
from the NAND flash.

239
00:10:23,140 --> 00:10:26,740
Then in phase 3, the
tertiary program loader,

240
00:10:26,740 --> 00:10:29,630
now we're at support

241
00:10:29,630 --> 00:10:31,845
of more of a
full-featured bootloader,

242
00:10:31,845 --> 00:10:33,720
something like U-boot
where we've got

243
00:10:33,720 --> 00:10:36,285
full support for reading
or writing NAND flash.

244
00:10:36,285 --> 00:10:38,340
It's able to read and

245
00:10:38,340 --> 00:10:40,650
decompress the
flattened device tree,

246
00:10:40,650 --> 00:10:42,240
the kernel, the initramfs,

247
00:10:42,240 --> 00:10:43,290
all the different
components that we

248
00:10:43,290 --> 00:10:44,950
need to actually boot kernel.

249
00:10:44,950 --> 00:10:49,365
We can take the kernel out of
something like NAND flash,

250
00:10:49,365 --> 00:10:52,430
including ECC, and
copy that into DRAM,

251
00:10:52,430 --> 00:10:54,465
and then jump into
and start the kernel.

252
00:10:54,465 --> 00:10:56,985
This phase 3 step

253
00:10:56,985 --> 00:10:59,250
gets us to exactly the point
at when we're starting

254
00:10:59,250 --> 00:11:01,785
our QMU instance using

255
00:11:01,785 --> 00:11:03,090
the simple examples that we've

256
00:11:03,090 --> 00:11:05,455
done with the first
part of the semester.

257
00:11:05,455 --> 00:11:07,770
Once we're at this point, we're

258
00:11:07,770 --> 00:11:10,000
ready to boot into the kernel.

259
00:11:10,770 --> 00:11:13,920
The tertiary program
loader hands

260
00:11:13,920 --> 00:11:16,325
over or jumps into the kernel,

261
00:11:16,325 --> 00:11:18,460
and now the kernel can
initialize all the memory in

262
00:11:18,460 --> 00:11:21,265
DRAM since the DRAM is
already set up for it.

263
00:11:21,265 --> 00:11:23,980
It can start the initramfs,

264
00:11:23,980 --> 00:11:25,985
where we can complete
hardware initialization,

265
00:11:25,985 --> 00:11:27,820
load some drivers if we need to,

266
00:11:27,820 --> 00:11:29,850
and set up that root file system

267
00:11:29,850 --> 00:11:31,920
so we can now access
the root filesystem and

268
00:11:31,920 --> 00:11:33,735
the NAND flash and mount it

269
00:11:33,735 --> 00:11:35,605
appropriately and boot and

270
00:11:35,605 --> 00:11:37,840
start running the Linux system.